195,0.0,0.0,-1.311301275563892e-06,0.0,-1.1920928244535389e-07,-0.0012698451755568385,-2.145764938177308e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,34193,429,220,1124,58,18,20,59,50853,19,15,28,16,19,15,15,7110,60,9885,1045,7546,400,77,3,448,400,15,59,273,80,308,27,16,18,24,24,3,1741,429,400,77,3,374,279,12557,65266,27949,311,220,16,21,15,59864,220,16,18,24,24,13,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nNotice that  \\[35\\cdot40=1400.\\]Find some integer $n$ with $0\\leq n<1399$ such that $n$ is the multiplicative inverse to 160 modulo 1399.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"0e14dcac73cf2d5f2613b6327a479b2d836f845b6c37d4f9ab49942cce215068"},"seconds":12.54234528541565,"seed":362380177602149062,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 0 \\leq n < 1399 \\) and \\( n \\) is the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399, we need to find \\( n \\) such that:\n\n\\[ 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\]\n\nThis means we need to find \\( n \\) such that \\( 160n - 1399k = 1 \\) for some integer \\( k \\). This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of 160 and 1399:\n\n1. \\( 1399 = 8 \\cdot 160 + 159 \\)\n2. \\( 160 = 1 \\cdot 159 + 1 \\)\n3. \\( 159 = 159 \\cdot 1 + 0 \\)\n\nThe gcd is 1, which means 160 and 1399 are coprime, and an inverse exists. Now we use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express 1 as a combination of 160 and 1399:\n\nFrom the second equation:\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot 159 \\]\n\nFrom the first equation, we substitute \\( 159 \\):\n\\[ 159 = 1399 - 8 \\cdot 160 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 1 = 160 - 1 \\cdot (1399 - 8 \\cdot 160) = 160 - 1399 + 8 \\cdot 160 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThus, we have:\n\\[ 1 = 9 \\cdot 160 - 1399 \\]\n\nThis tells us that \\( n = 9 \\) is a solution to the equation \\( 160n \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1399} \\). Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of 160 modulo 1399 is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{9} \\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{9}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:231"}
